 User wants to find the value of the sum $S=\sum_{k=0}^{49}(-1)^k\binom{99}{2k}$.
To simplify the expression, the User writes the first few terms of the sum as follows:
\begin{align*}
S &=\binom{99}{0} -\binom{99}{2}+\binom{99}{4}-\binom{99}{6}+\cdots +\binom{99}{96} - \binom{99}{98}\\
&=\binom{99}{0} +\binom{99}{98}-\left[\binom{99}{2}+\binom{99}{98}\right]+\left[\binom{99}{4}+\binom{99}{98}\right]+\cdots+\binom{99}{50}
\end{align*}
because $\binom{99}{i}=\binom{99}{99-i}$ for all integers $i$ belonging to the set $\{0,1,\dots, 99\}$.
The User then realizes that the two terms in each parenthetical bracket can be simplified using the identity $\binom{n}{k}+\binom{n}{k-1}=\binom{n+1}{k}$.
For example,
\begin{align*}
\binom{99}{2}+\binom{99}{98} &=\binom{100}{3}\\
\binom{99}{4}+\binom{99}{98} &=\binom{100}{5}\\
&\quad \vdots\\
\binom{99}{50} &=\binom{100}{51}
\end{align*}

<answer>
\begin{align*}
S &= \binom{99}{0} +\binom{99}{98} - \binom{100}{3} + \binom{100}{5} - \cdots + \binom{100}{49}\\
&= \binom{99}{0} - \binom{100}{3} + \binom{100}{5} - \cdots + \binom{100}{49}\\
&= (-1)^0 \binom{99}{0} + (-1)^1 \binom{100}{3} + (-1)^2 \binom{100}{5} + \cdots + (-1)^{25} \binom{100}{49}\\
&= 2^{50}
\end{align*}
Therefore, the value of $S$ is $\boxed{2^{50}}$.
The answer is (E).

